# mgmaya.msg - Mother Maya of the Children of Atom (mgmaya.ssl).
# 100-105 look, 110-129 floating remarks, 200+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a woman in patched robes, trimming candle wicks.}
{101}{}{You see Mother Maya. She looks as if she would like everyone to sit down and be quiet.}
{102}{}{Her hair is white and thicker than it has any right to be. Her hands are never still: a wick, a hem, a crumb off the altar.}
{105}{}{No. Oh, no. Not in here. Not you.}

# 110-117: any day.
{110}{}{He does go on. Twenty years, and I still listen. That's love.}
{111}{}{Somebody has tracked glow-water across the floor again.}
{112}{}{Candles don't dip themselves.}
{113}{}{Atom keep you, and keep you from the stew at Moriarty's.}
{114}{}{So much shouting in this town. He only wants quiet. They both do.}
{115}{}{I hem his robes shorter every year. The water takes the rest.}
{116}{}{If the Division comes on a wash day, I shall be very cross.}
{117}{}{Sit, if you like. Nobody does. I always offer.}

# 120-122: the bomb is disarmed.
{120}{}{It's quieter by the pool. I never knew it hummed.}
{121}{}{He preaches louder now. I think he's grieving.}
{122}{}{A god with His eyes shut is still a god. And easier to dust.}

# 125-126: Simms is dead.
{125}{}{Lucas, dead. A gun did that. A gun always does that.}
{126}{}{I'll take the boy some soup. Soup is all I know how to do.}

# 128-129: Burke's charge is fitted.
{128}{}{My teeth ache. They only do that before storms.}
{129}{}{The brahmin won't drink today. Brahmin know things.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{Come in, come in. Mind the pews; they bite. I'm Maya. Mother Maya, if my husband is listening. He's the one out there shouting at the water.}
{201}{}{Back again? Sit. Nobody ever does, but I always offer.}
{202}{}{You've heard about Lucas. All that noise in the world, and then one small sound, and a good man is gone. I will never understand what people see in guns.}
{203}{}{So it's done, and the bomb is quiet. My husband took it hard. I took it with tea. Sit down, dear.}
{204}{}{Yes, dear?}
{205}{}{May I ask something else?}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{What do the Children of Atom believe?}
{211}{}{What's the news in town?}
{212}{}{Doesn't the radiation worry you?}
{213}{}{How does your church welcome a stranger?}
{214}{}{Lady glow?}
{215}{}{Atom keep you, Mother.}
{216}{}{Bye, nice lady.}

# Doctrine.
{220}{}{Oh, ask my husband. He has the words; I have the washing. Atom is in everything, the bomb is Atom having a nap, and one day we all become a great many people at once. I hope some of them get to sit down.}

# The news.
{230}{}{Only what everyone whispers. The sheriff wants the bomb made safe, and he'd pay good money to whoever can do it. Nobody here can tell a fuse from a fork. Don't tell my husband I said so; he'd make a sermon of it.}
{231}{}{Would that upset you?}
{232}{}{Me? I'd sleep better. I love Atom. I would love Him every bit as much asleep for good.}
{233}{}{They say the sheriff has found somebody to see to the bomb at last. A stranger. Oh. Well. I'll put the kettle on for afterwards; my husband will need it.}
{234}{}{The news is you, dear. The bomb is quiet, the sheriff is smiling, and the Stahls are arguing over who gets to sell the first "safe at last" stew.}
{236}{}{Poor Lucas wanted that bomb made safe; it was all he talked about. They say the money he put by is still at his house, with the boy. Somebody could earn it yet. I'd like that, for Lucas.}
{235}{}{Nothing new. Only the brahmin won't drink today, and my husband says the water sang to him. He has said that before. He has never looked frightened before.}

# The radiation.
{240}{}{It did, the first year. Then my hair came back in white and thicker than before, and I thought: well. The doctor shouts at us. I bring him soup, and he shouts with his mouth full.}

# The welcome cup.
{250}{}{With a cup from the pool, dear. It's how we greet people. It's warm, it glows a little, and it tastes of pennies. Will you?}
{251}{}{[Drink it.]}
{252}{}{The water is warm and tastes of pennies. Your teeth hum for a while afterwards.}
{253}{}{There! One of us, for an afternoon. If your gums tingle, that's only Him saying hello.}
{254}{}{I'll pass.}
{255}{}{Sensible. I'm told it's an acquired taste. I'm told that mostly by Doc Church.}
{256}{}{One cup to a pilgrim, dear. Atom is generous, but the doctor counts.}

# Low Intelligence.
{260}{}{A little, dear, around the edges. Here, have a cracker. No, that's a candle. This one.}
{261}{}{Still a little, dear. No more crackers; you've had the cracker. Mind the step on your way out.}
